      <h3>Top Answer: What is Daylight Saving Time (DST)?</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Daylight Saving Time (DST) is the seasonal practice of advancing civil clocks forward by one hour during spring and summer months, then setting them back one hour in autumn.</strong> 
        The primary objective is to shift an hour of natural daylight from the early morning (when most people are asleep) to the evening (when social and commercial activity peaks). 
        While observed by approximately 40% of countries worldwide—including the US, Canada, EU members, and parts of Australia—DST is not universal. High-latitude regions benefit most from evening daylight extension, whereas equatorial regions experience consistent daylight year-round and maintain permanent standard time.
      </p>
