  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <section id="core-mechanics" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Core Mechanics of DST">
    <h2>1. Core Mechanics: Spring Forward &amp; Fall Back</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time (DST) operates on a straightforward mechanical shift: advancing local clocks by 60 minutes relative to the standard astronomical time offset. When DST begins in the spring, civil time jumps from 2:00 AM directly to 3:00 AM. This transition reduces that single day to 23 hours, causing people to "lose" an hour of sleep. Conversely, when DST ends in autumn, local clocks jump from 2:00 AM back to 1:00 AM, repeating that 60-minute window and creating a 25-hour day where people "gain" an hour.
    </p>
    <p>
      This seasonal realignment directly impacts regional time zone offsets. For example, during winter standard time, New York operates on <a href="/blog/what-is-est/">Eastern Standard Time (EST)</a>, which is UTC-5 (or <a href="/gmt-vs-est/">GMT-5</a>). When Daylight Saving Time takes effect in March, New York shifts to Eastern Daylight Time (EDT), changing its offset to UTC-4. For an in-depth comparison of these two phases, consult our guide on <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Similarly, London shifts from <a href="/blog/what-is-gmt/">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0)</a> in winter to British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1) in summer. When calculating global time differences—such as using a <a href="/gmt-to-est/">GMT to EST converter</a> or an <a href="/est-to-gmt/">EST to GMT converter</a>—understanding whether both regions are on standard time or daylight time is essential to preventing scheduling errors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2: VISUAL DIAGRAM -->
